# Airness Payload Layer Guide

This guide applies to every module that inherits `airness-parent-payload`. It adds to the generic guide and to
the node rules; it never relaxes them. A Payload module is a node project whose `package.json` declares
`payload` and `ploaness`, built with `pom` packaging.

## Verification

`mvn clean verify` gives the Default verdict and `mvn clean verify -Pextended` the Extended one. The Payload
gates run through `airness:node-gate` like every node gate, bound by `airness-parent-payload`:

| Gate | Phase | What it checks |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `preflight` | `process-sources` | The node project holds a Payload application, and every Payload application declares `payload`, `graphql`, `sharp`, `next`, `react` and `react-dom` at the versions the layer pins; every `@payloadcms/*` package is at the Payload version. |
| `payload-blocklist` | `post-integration-test` | The lockfile resolves no package the Payload layer refuses: the MongoDB adapter, and the drivers of database servers Payload has no adapter for. |
| `payload-source-rules` | `post-integration-test` | Local API calls state their bounds, thread `req`, and decide access; every collection and global decides its access; auth collections lock accounts; uploads are restricted; a configuration that pushes no schema records its migrations; nothing writes at boot. |
| `admin-views` | `post-integration-test` | Every custom admin view has an accessibility specification of the project's own. |
| `payload-environment` | `post-integration-test` | Every variable `src/lib/environment.ts` reads is documented in the repository's example file, and every `NEXT_PUBLIC_` variable it inlines is declared by an `ARG` in each Dockerfile that builds the member. |
| `payload-generated` | `post-integration-test` | Regenerating the Payload types and the admin import map with the project's own Payload CLI leaves them unchanged. |
| `payload-defaults` | `post-integration-test` | Every collection and global of the built configuration, framework-built ones included, decides its access; no drafts read serves an unapproved document to a stranger; production authentication limits are valid; `admin.autoLogin` is off. |

Run one gate with `pnpm exec ploaness gate <id> --verbose` to diagnose it. That is a diagnosis, never a verdict.

The node gates apply the Payload layer's additions too. The `eslint` gate runs the node rules plus the Payload
blocks below, and the Extended `e2e` gate runs the layer's `access-boundary` specification, which asks Payload's
`/api/access` what an anonymous caller may do and fails on every grant `publicAccess` does not declare.

## Settings

The Payload settings live under the `ploaness` key of the member's `package.json`; the node settings stay
under `airness`.

| Setting | Default | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `publicAccess` | empty | Entries with `entity`, `operation`, `reason` and optional `fields`, declaring the anonymous permissions the project grants on purpose. An entry missing a field is dropped. |
| `analysisEnv` | placeholders for `PAYLOAD_SECRET` and `DATABASE_URL` | Variables the gates that import the configuration use when neither the process nor `.env.local`/`.env` supplies them. They let the configuration evaluate; nothing connects to them. |

The layer contributes defaults to the node settings of every member that declares `ploaness`: the generated
files (`src/payload-types.ts`, `src/payload-generated-schema.ts`, and everything under `src/app/(payload)`, the
admin and API glue Payload scaffolds as generated), the framework glue (`src/payload.config.ts`), the pure-logic floor (`src/access`, `src/lib`)
and the routes the accessibility sweep leaves to Payload (`/admin`, `/api`). Agents may not edit the generated
files; regenerate them with the Payload CLI.

## Application Code

- Keep environment reads in `src/lib/environment.ts`, validate them there, and document each variable in the
  example file at the repository root.
- Collection, global, field and block definitions are declarative. Define access rules and pure logic in
  `src/access` or `src/lib`, hooks in `src/hooks`, and import them by reference.
- A collection decides `create`, `read`, `update` and `delete`; a global decides `read` and `update`.
- At a Local API call use an explicit options object. A read states its `depth` or `limit`; a call through
  `req.payload` passes `req`; a call carrying `user`, a call composed with a spread, and every call in an
  endpoint module or route handler states `overrideAccess: false` after any spread.
- A database adapter that does not push its schema needs a recorded migration for every schema change.
- ESLint refuses an inline function in a collection, global, field or block configuration, and a read of
  `process.env` outside `src/lib/environment.ts` and `src/proxy.ts`. A hook writes request state only on
  `req.context`. A migration keeps the snake-case name `payload migrate:create` gives it.
- Keep Payload's scaffold under `src/app/(payload)` exactly as Payload writes it. It is a generated artefact:
  the gates leave it to Payload, and agents may not edit it.

## Application verification setup

Declare service preparation and the disposable-data wrapper under `airness.pretest` and `airness.testWrapper`.
These run for tests, production builds and the browser suite. Keep Payload-specific access declarations under
`ploaness.publicAccess`; the generic settings and npm vulnerability mitigations belong under `airness`.
The installed layer contributes exact security replacements for affected Payload dependency edges through
`airness.layer.securityOverrides`. Projects may use only those approvals and the generic Airness approvals.
